{"hash":"7e75e301c3b7d938","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":101.86,"said":"Stay a little longer in the sanctum, because the most tender moment of Omkareshwar’s day comes at night. Devotees believe that Shiva roams the three worlds all day long, and every night he comes here, to this island, to rest, with Mata Parvati beside him. And so, each night, the Lord is put to bed. At about half past nine the Shayan Aarti begins, the aarti of rest, sung softly, the lamps moving slowly. Then the priests set out the night’s arrangements in the sanctum: a silver swing, a bed with fine bedding, a chamar fan, a damru, and a chaupar board with its dice. Chaupar is the old Indian game of dice and pieces, and the story goes that before they sleep, Shiva and Parvati sit down to play. For a short while after the aarti, devotees may come for shayan darshan and see it all laid out. Then the doors are closed for the night. And here is the part people love to tell: it is said that when the doors open in the morning, the dice and pieces lie scattered, as though someone had played through the night. Take it as a miracle or as a loving story, but think of what it means. Here God is not only worshipped; he is welcomed home each night, like family. Devotees may even offer a whole new set of bedding and clothes for Bholenath and Mataji. Fold your hands, and wish the Lord a peaceful night.\n\nDid you know? The temple trust’s own timetable lists it: Shayan Arti from 9:30 to 10:00 pm, then Shayan Darshan, a view of the night’s arrangements, from 10:00 to 10:30 pm.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1306},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1322,"end":1480}],"words":[[0.098,0.325,0,4],[0.435,0.048,5,6],[0.495,0.312,7,13],[0.819,0.445,14,20],[1.276,0.096,21,23],[1.384,0.096,24,27],[1.492,0.637,28,35],[2.346,0.325,37,44],[2.682,0.096,45,48],[2.79,0.361,49,53],[3.163,0.325,54,60],[3.499,0.409,61,67],[3.92,0.168,68,70],[4.101,0.817,71,84],[4.93,0.168,85,88],[5.11,0.312,89,94],[5